## Phase 2: Brutal Specific Questions for the Engineering Team

### Q1. Weight Learning Lookahead Bias
> In [`weight_learning_service.py:L42-L52`](file:///c:/Users/Vipul%20Gorana/Desktop/Config%20Manager/Dummy/backend/app/services/weight_learning_service.py#L42-L52), the `ElasticNetCV` uses `KFold(n_splits=5, shuffle=False)` on forward return data. However, the `factor_scores_df` and `forward_returns` are passed from the **already-realized** cross-sectional universe. Where is the temporal purge? A standard `KFold` on financial data creates massive train-test information overlap. Why isn't `PurgedWalkForwardCV` used here?

### Q2. Backtest Strategy Close-to-Close Execution
> In [`quant_service.py:L214-L216`](file:///c:/Users/Vipul%20Gorana/Desktop/Config%20Manager/Dummy/backend/app/services/quant_service.py#L214-L216), Strategy A (PEAD) enters at `e_day['Close']` and exits at `df.iloc[iloc_idx + 20]['Close']`. This assumes you can execute at the **exact closing auction price** on the earnings day itself — the most volatile bar of the quarter. Why isn't the entry at **Next-Day Open** like Strategy D, which is the only realistic execution assumption?

### Q3. Static 0.40% Transaction Cost Across All Strategies
> In [`quant_service.py:L216`](file:///c:/Users/Vipul%20Gorana/Desktop/Config%20Manager/Dummy/backend/app/services/quant_service.py#L216), a static `0.40%` friction deduction is applied to every strategy. The momentum backtest engine (`backtest_service.py`) uses a sophisticated market-impact model (`estimate_slippage`) with ADV-based participation rate modeling. Why do the event strategy backtests use a flat, unrealistically low friction while the ML momentum backtest uses a proper impact model? The two engines produce incomparable metrics.

### Q4. Survivorship Bias Acknowledged But Not Mitigated in Universe Sync
> In [`universe_service.py:L32-L33`](file:///c:/Users/Vipul%20Gorana/Desktop/Config%20Manager/Dummy/backend/app/services/universe_service.py#L32-L33), the comment literally says *"this accepts survivorship bias for historical backtesting in exchange for providing a comprehensive, realistic current universe"*. The `sync_historical_constituents()` only seeds from the **current** NSE listing. Delisted companies are never back-populated. How do you justify any historical backtest metric when the universe itself is contaminated by survivorship bias?

### Q5. PSI Drift Detection Computed But Never Acted Upon
> In [`ml_service.py:L257-L279`](file:///c:/Users/Vipul%20Gorana/Desktop/Config%20Manager/Dummy/backend/app/services/ml_service.py#L257-L279), the `calculate_psi_drift()` method is implemented. But where is it ever called? Searching the codebase shows zero invocations. The model never detects regime drift and never triggers retraining. The method is dead code.

### Q6. CalibratedClassifierCV Is Using Purged Splits But Gets Passed an Integer Fallback
> In [`ml_service.py:L231-L244`](file:///c:/Users/Vipul%20Gorana/Desktop/Config%20Manager/Dummy/backend/app/services/ml_service.py#L231-L244), the `splits = list(cv_gen.split(...))` can return fewer than 3 splits, in which case `splits` is reassigned to the **integer `3`** (L234). Then `CalibratedClassifierCV(base_pipeline, cv=3)` silently falls back to **standard KFold(3)**, completely destroying the purged walk-forward discipline. This is a latent lookahead bias bomb that fires when data is insufficient.

### Q7. `min_periods=1` Rolling Averages Create NaN-Free But Statistically Meaningless Warm-Up Values
> In [`backtest_service.py:L135-L141`](file:///c:/Users/Vipul%20Gorana/Desktop/Config%20Manager/Dummy/backend/app/services/backtest_service.py#L135-L141), `SMA50`, `SMA200`, and `ATR` are calculated with `min_periods=1`. This means the first bar's "200-day moving average" is literally just that bar's close price. The trading logic then uses these warm-up values to make entry/exit decisions from `start_idx = min(50, ...)`. Why isn't the trading window strictly gated to `>= 200` bars to avoid nonsensical indicator values?

### Q8. HML Factor Hardcoded to 0.0 in Factor Attribution
> In [`factor_attribution_service.py:L48`](file:///c:/Users/Vipul%20Gorana/Desktop/Config%20Manager/Dummy/backend/app/services/factor_attribution_service.py#L48), `ff_df['HML'] = 0.0`. The entire Value factor is zeroed out. This means any OLS alpha regression against this "Fama-French 3-Factor" model is actually a 2-factor model that systematically misattributes value premium as alpha. Any reported "alpha" is inflated.

### Q9. AI Fallback Report Contains Fabricated Backtest Statistics
> In [`ai_analyst_service.py:L312-L317`](file:///c:/Users/Vipul%20Gorana/Desktop/Config%20Manager/Dummy/backend/app/services/ai_analyst_service.py#L312-L317), the deterministic fallback `_generate_fallback_report()` hardcodes synthetic backtest performance: `PEAD 64.2% win rate (14 trades)`, `Mean Reversion 58.5% (18 trades)`, etc. These numbers are **completely fabricated** — they are not derived from any actual data. They are presented to the user as real analytics.

### Q10. Benchmark Proxy in Hypothesis Engine Uses Constant Risk-Free Rate Instead of Market Returns
> In [`hypothesis_service.py:L337`](file:///c:/Users/Vipul%20Gorana/Desktop/Config%20Manager/Dummy/backend/app/services/hypothesis_service.py#L337), the White's Reality Check benchmark is `np.zeros_like(s_arr) + 0.0002` — a flat 0.02% daily constant. This severely biases the Reality Check test because a proper benchmark should be the Nifty 50 market return, not a risk-free proxy. During bull markets, almost any momentum strategy trivially beats a flat 0.02% benchmark, making the test meaningless.

